{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>But you who cling, etc.</b> One needs to know why it says <b>and you</b> with the addition of the vav, as though adding to a prior matter — and not comparing those who cleave to HaShem to those who went after Baal Peor. Further, it said <b>the-dveikim</b> with the definite article, yet we find no prior mention of this dveikut (cleaving) that would justify saying ha-dveikim (the ones who cleave) with the demonstrative hey." "<b>Or perhaps</b> it may be explained along the lines of what they, may their memory be for a blessing, expounded in Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 108a) on the verse ״וְנֹחַ מָצָא חֵן בְּעֵינֵי ה׳״ (Genesis 6:8), and this is their language: ״וְנֹחַ״ — even Noah, who survived from among them, would not have been worthy, except that he found favor, end quote. So too the statement <b>and you</b> intends: even you were worthy of being destroyed, except that by virtue of your being dveikim — meaning, you drew near in repentance and cleaved to HaShem — in this you live." "And it was precise to say <b>all of you</b>, to signal that He is speaking not about a portion of them but about all of them. And so the verse states in the portion of Pinchas, regarding the incident of Peor (Numbers 25:11): ״וְלֹא כִלִּיתִי אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּקִנְאָתִי״ — from this you learn that all of them stood to be annihilated. And although Pinchas was the one who averted their destruction, nonetheless it is compelling that they repented afterward, for if not, Pinchas would have availed only to atone for what they had already done, but not for what was yet to come." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend through the added vav, according to what I explained in connection with His saying ״אֲשֶׁר הָלַךְ אַחֲרֵי בַעַל״ etc. — that it intends to say that even though one did not serve but merely thought in his mind to serve — the vav of <b>and you</b> intends to add to the first comparison: just as HaShem punished for the thought in what preceded, even though no deed was done, so too regarding your dveikut in HaShem — the Holy One, blessed be He, joins the thought to the deed. And so they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Kiddushin 40a): a good thought — the Holy One, blessed be He, joins it to the deed; an evil thought — the Holy One, blessed be He, does not join it to the deed, except in the case of idolatry." "And it was precise to say <b>and you, the-dveikim</b>, which may be explained along the lines of their saying, may their memory be for a blessing (Berakhot 7a), on the verse (Exodus 33:16) ״וְנִפְלִינוּ אֲנִי וְעַמְּךָ״ — that Moses asked of HaShem that He not cause His Shekhinah to rest upon the nations of the world, and we explained there that the intention is even if they make their deeds proper. And that is what it says here, ״you״ — meaning, you are the ones who are dveikim in HaShem and not the idol worshippers; for if the idol worshpires wished to become dveikim in HaShem they would not merit this. And along this line, the statement ״ha-dveikim״ is a grammatical determination [of identity] also for the statement ״you.״" "<b>You who cleave to HaShem your Elohim.</b> It may be explained based on what Rambam wrote in chapter 6 of Hilkhot Yesodei HaTorah, and this is his language: ״And there are seven names: the Tetragrammaton, etc. Whoever erases even one letter of these seven names is liable to lashes. Any letter attached to HaShem's name from before it (as a prefix) is permitted to be erased, etc.; from after it, such as the ך׳ of אֱלֹהֶיךָ, the ם׳ of אֱלֹהֵיכֶם — these are not erased, and they are like the rest of the letters of the Name, for HaShem sanctifies them,״ end quote. And this is what He intended to say to them in the phrase <b>who cleave to HaShem</b> — meaning, since this unique name, the name of the Tetragrammaton, has no letter attached or joined to it from after it but only from before it, such as לַה׳, בַּה׳, כַּה׳, and these letters hold no sanctity and are permitted to be erased — if so, Israel's cleaving to HaShem would be in this manner, that the Name does not sanctify them. Therefore He concluded <b>your Elohim</b> — meaning, this cleaving of yours, by which you cleave to HaShem, is not like the cleaving of the letters before it, but rather like the letters after it, which are the ם׳ of אֱלֹהֵיכֶם, which are holy like the rest of the letters of the Name." "<b>Furthermore,</b> in the word <b>בַּה׳</b> He hinted at a pleasant allusion, namely that this name does not sanctify what follows it. From this you have learned that the cleaving of Israel is to the names themselves, as the light of their soul unifies with the light of the honored Name. And so that you not understand the intention to be like the letters attached from before it, which have no sanctity, He concluded <b>your Elohim.</b> Say from now on that the intention of His saying <b>בַּה׳</b> is in the unification of the letters, and this is what the verse says (Deuteronomy 32:9): ״כִּי חֵלֶק ה׳ עַמּוֹ״ — see, Israel is one portion of the name of the Tetragrammaton, blessed be He, and by this you will justify this our interpretation. Or perhaps the verse speaks against two levels that exist in Israel, one above the other, and understand:" "<b>Further,</b> He intended in the phrase <b>who cleave to HaShem your Elohim</b> in the manner of what they said, of blessed memory (Zohar III 138a), that one who performs a mitzvah causes the name of the Tetragrammaton to rest upon the limb with which he performs the mitzvah. And they said that positive commandments are alluded to in the name of the Tetragrammaton and negative commandments are alluded to in the name Elohim — and this is His saying here <b>and you who cleave</b>, through the observance of positive and negative commandments in these two names: the name of the Tetragrammaton and Elohim, as He says: ״בַּה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend on this path: when you are cleaving to HaShem — meaning, your heart desires Him and His commandments — by this He is called your Elohim in unification, in the manner of what they said, of blessed memory (Yalkut Shim'oni, end of Parashat Noah), on the verse ״יוֹשֵׁב תְּהִלּוֹת יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Psalms 22:4), that HaShem was not satisfied with all the praise of the hosts of heaven until they said in His praises ״בָּרוּךְ ה׳ אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — for He did not choose that His divinity be unified except upon Israel. And therefore you will find that He is not blessed nor praised as Elohei heaven, nor as Elohei earth, nor as Elohei angels, but rather as Elohei Israel." "<b>All of you alive today.</b> This is drawn upward and downward. Drawn upward, on this path: your Elohim [are] life, for HaShem is called (Deuteronomy 5:23) Elohim living. And drawn downward as well: all of you [are] alive. And the intention in the measurement of the utterance, on this path, is to awaken [the awareness] that life is drawn to them from the Source of life. And the intention in this is that anything other than this is not called life, even if it moves about and eats, etc., for the wicked in their lifetime are called dead (Berakhot 18b), inasmuch as their source is the source of death. And this is the novelty that Moses innovates for them in this utterance — that he informs them that they are alive from the Source of life." "<b>And the measure</b> of the intent of Moses' words is in two ways. First, on this path — from the side that you are cleaving to HaShem your Elohim, you are alive — alive, as we have written nearby. Second, on this path — the reason you merited to cleave to HaShem your Elohim is because you are alive, meaning righteous, for you guarded yourselves from the enticements of the wicked, which are in the category of death, and you chose life — and the verse intended both of them." "<b>And its saying 'all of you'</b> — it intends the inclusiveness of the individual and the inclusiveness of the collective, on this path: the inclusiveness of the individual — that each one of them was entirely alive, with no deficient limb, along the lines of what they expounded in Ḥullin (Zevaḥim 116a) concerning the verse ״מִכָּל הַחַי״ — bring an animal whose life is in all its limbs and which has no blemish. So too in our case, they were all whole, without blemish. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Mekhilta) that all who stood at Mount Sinai had no blindness in them, as it is written ״וְכָל הָעָם רוֹאִים״ etc., and not etc., end. From this you have learned that they had no blemishes and stood in their full strength. And it intended both blemishes of the body and blemishes of the soul. And I have already noted that a blemish in the body testifies to a flaw in the soul, for there is no blemish that does not have its counterpart in the soul, and therefore HaShem disqualifies a blemished person from the altar. And see the words of the Zohar (vol. III 4a). The inclusiveness of the collective — that there was among them not a single one who was unfit, who would not be called alive, but all were righteous. And its saying <b>'today'</b> — it did not establish the statement that they are all alive as permanent, but only today, for who can say what will be tomorrow on account of their deeds, since a person has free choice and there is no reliance on him until the day he goes to the land of the living."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {11 "The Talmudic citation reads 'חולין' (Ḥullin) but gives the page reference 'זבחים קטז.' (Zevaḥim 116a); translated exactly as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:42.470714991Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}